[19:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that the camera is locked. We\'re going to make sure there\'s no music at all, only dialogue and sound design. That\'s an important step here. Otherwise, you\'re going to be fighting random music that\'s going to be baked into your video, literally, and you can\'t do'

[19:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything about it. Let\'s do that. We\'re going to get that started, and we\'ll see you back here as we get stuff going. So, I\'ve got all these different videos generated now. Some other ones, we are doing some iPhone footage. So, I made'

[20:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sure to specifically say I want some of these videos to be captured as if it were on an iPhone. So, it\'s going to do some different movements. It\'s going to feel more authentic to being an iPhone. What you\'re going to notice is I\'m not'

[20:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doing a ton of image reference sheets as our starter frame. A lot of times when I use start frames, I will start to notice some more plasticiness or some fakeness that comes through the video. Most of'

[20:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the time, if I just tell the model, I want this person in this setting, and it knows what the setting is like with the haze and whatnot, it\'s going to feel a lot more authentic of where the person is in that setting. But if there\'s a'

[20:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'specific thing, like you want your character to be sitting on a specific stool in a kitchen and you don\'t want them to move, that\'s when you would use a start frame reference image so that it knows in that whole plate where that'

[21:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character is sitting. And then what I would do is not start from that frame. I would start from maybe a closeup or a different angle just so you have more dynamic depth when you\'re building out those scenes. So, we\'ve got our'

[21:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
